A local class is a class declared inside a function. It is visible only within the function in which it is declared and is commonly used when a class is required only for a specific function.Accessible only within its enclosing function.Helps keep implementation details local and avoids unnecessary global classes.Example: The following program demonstrates creating a local class inside a function.
            CPP

    #include <iostream> 
using namespace std; 
void fun() 
{ 
    class Test { 
    public: 
        void display() 
        { 
            cout << "Local Class"; 

        } 

    }; 

    Test t; 
    t.display(); 

} 

int main() 
{ 
    fun(); 
    return 0; 

}

OutputLocal ClassExplanationTest is declared inside the function fun().The class is visible only inside fun().An object of Test is created and used within the same function.Syntaxvoid functionName() {            class ClassName {         // class members            };             ClassName obj; }Characteristics of Local ClassesLocal classes have several rules that distinguish them from regular classes.Scope of a Local ClassThe name of a local class is visible only inside the function where it is declared.Objects of the class can only be created inside the enclosing function. The class name cannot be used outside the function. Example: The following program shows that a local class can only be used inside its enclosing function.
            CPP

    #include <iostream>
using namespace std;

void fun()
{
    // Local class
    class Test {
        // Body
    };

    Test t; // Fine
    Test* tp; // Fine
}

int main()
{
    Test t; // Error
    Test* tp; // Error
    return 0;
}

Explanation: Test is declared inside fun(), so its scope is limited to that function. Attempting to create an object of Test outside fun() results in a compilation error.Member Function DefinitionAll member functions of a local class must be defined inside the class definition.Member functions cannot be defined outside the local class. Defining them outside the class results in a compilation error.Example (Valid): The following program demonstrates a local class with all member functions defined inside the class.
            CPP

    #include <iostream>
using namespace std;

void fun()
{
    class Test // local to fun
    {
    public:
        // Fine as the method is defined
        // inside the local class
        void method()
        {
            cout << "Local Class method() called";
        }
    };

    Test t;
    t.method();
}

int main()
{
    fun();
    return 0;
}

OutputLocal Class method() calledExample (Invalid): The following program demonstrates a compilation error caused by defining a local class member function outside the class.
            CPP

    #include <iostream>
using namespace std;

void fun()
{
    class Test // local to fun
    {
    public:
        void method();
    };

    // Error as the method is defined outside the local
    // class
    void Test::method() { cout << "Local Class method()"; }
}

int main() { return 0; }

Output Compiler Error: In function 'void fun()': error: a function-definition is not allowed here before '{' tokenStatic MembersLocal classes have restrictions on static members.Static data members are not allowed. Static member functions are allowed. Example (Invalid): The following program attempts to declare a static data member in a local class, resulting in a compilation error.
            CPP

    #include <iostream>
using namespace std;

void fun()
{
    class Test // local to fun
    {
        static int i;
    };
}

int main() { return 0; }

OutputCompiler Error: In function 'void fun()': error: local class 'class fun()::Test' shall not have static data member 'int fun()::Test::i'Example (Valid): The following program demonstrates that a local class can contain static member functions.
            CPP

    #include <iostream>
using namespace std;

void fun()
{
    class Test // local to fun
    {
    public:
        static void method()
        {
            cout << "Local Class method() called";
        }
    };

    Test::method();
}

int main()
{
    fun();
    return 0;
}

OutputLocal Class method() calledAccessing Variables of the Enclosing FunctionMember functions of a local class have limited access to variables declared in the enclosing function.Static variables can be accessed. Enumeration constants can be accessed. Non-static local variables cannot be accessed directly. Example (Valid): The following program demonstrates accessing static and enumeration variables from a local class.
            CPP

    #include <iostream>
using namespace std;

void fun()
{
    static int x;
    enum { i = 1, j = 2 };

    // Local class
    class Test {
    public:
        void method()
        {
            cout << "x = " << x
                 << endl; // fine as x is static
            cout << "i = " << i
                 << endl; // fine as i is enum
        }
    };

    Test t;
    t.method();
}

int main()
{
    fun();
    return 0;
}

Outputx = 0
i = 1Example (Invalid): The following program demonstrates a compilation error caused by accessing a non-static local variable of the enclosing function from a local class.
            CPP

    #include <iostream>
using namespace std;

void fun()
{
    int x;

    // Local class
    class Test {
    public:
        void method() { cout << "x = " << x << endl; }
    };

    Test t;
    t.method();
}

int main()
{
    fun();
    return 0;
}

OutputCompiler Error: use of local variable with automatic storage from containing functionAccessing Global Entities Local classes can access entities outside the enclosing function.Global variables and functions can be accessed. Global types can be used. Other local classes declared within the same function can also be accessed.Example: The following program demonstrates accessing global entities and another local class from within a local class.
            CPP

    #include <iostream>
using namespace std;

int x;

void fun()
{

    // First Local class
    class Test1 {
    public:
        Test1() { cout << "Test1::Test1()" << endl; }
    };

    // Second Local class
    class Test2 {
        // Fine: A local class can use other local classes
        // of same function
        Test1 t1;

    public:
        void method()
        {
            // Fine: Local class member methods can access
            // global variables.
            cout << "x = " << x << endl;
        }
    };

    Test2 t;
    t.method();
}

int main()
{
    fun();
    return 0;
}

OutputTest1::Test1()
x = 0ExplanationTest2 uses another local class Test1. The local class accesses the global variable x. Local classes can interact with global entities just like regular classes.Limitations of Local ClassesLocal classes have several restrictions compared to regular classes.Can only be used within the enclosing function. Member functions must be defined inside the class. Static data members are not allowed. Cannot directly access non-static local variables of the enclosing function.Related Article: Nested Classes in C++
